    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d542">

                <pb facs="0680" n="666" sameAs="frus:frus1943v06/pg666"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711F.1914/738</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. Murray M. Wise of the Division of
                        the American Republics</hi><anchor n="46" xml:id="d542-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-09-03">September 3, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In accordance with instructions from the Department,<anchor n="47"
                        xml:id="d542-f2"/> Ambassador Wilson has clearly stated to the Panamanian
                    Foreign Minister this Government’s position with respect to a change in the site
                    of the Panama railroad station and yards. Dr. Fábrega says he cannot see why
                    these particular lots should not be included in the deed of conveyance now under
                    preparation. He explained that his government was not especially interested in
                    seeing the station moved at this time but that it was feared that unless the
                    station lots were included in the present deed, the whole question of their
                    transfer at some future time would have to be taken up with our Congress. Amb.
                    Wilson said his instructions definitely disposed of that point by emphatically
                    stating that any transfer of these lands would require Congressional
                    approval.</p>
                <p>The Minister said he would report our position to the President. Speaking
                    laughingly he said the Panamanians were obstinate negotiators and probably would
                    insist on their point of view. The Ambassador said he was aware of Panamanian
                    obstinacy but hoped that the Minister would advise the President that the U.S.
                    Gov’t had spoken definitively.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
